Newport decorated console: manual emulator/hardware regression
=============================================================

For implementation guidance and extending this to other framebuffer drivers,
see README.decor-porting in this directory.

Build the sgimips GENERIC32_IP2x kernel configuration (or include it in a
local configuration).  Boot an Indy/IP24 or Indigo2/IP22 with Newport graphics
and PROM console=g.  XZ/GR2 is a separate driver and is not decorated by this
option.
NEWPORT_DECOR and white kernel messages are enabled by default in
GENERIC32_IP2x, GENERIC64_IP2x, and GENERIC64_32_IP2x.  The named NEWPORT_DECOR
configuration inherits the same defaults from GENERIC32_IP2x.
The gradient uses 112 palette levels from black to #008060.  Text colours and
their inverse cursor colours are reserved; every gradient entry also has an
inverse cursor entry.  The default white boot messages ensure that
they remain legible at the green end of the screen.

Use disposable disk/NVRAM copies with Iris and Prismatic.  Keep graphics
enabled: Iris --ci provides offscreen graphics, whereas --headless disables
them.  Prismatic can run under Xvfb.  Boot via the PROM with TFTP or a boot
disk, so that firmware initializes the display before NetBSD attaches.

On the guest, compile and run as root on ttyE0:

    cc -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror newport_decor.c -o newport_decor
    ./newport_decor

An optional progress-file argument records READY (with rows/columns) and
DONE for each command.  With a graphical console, /dev/ttyZ0 can send these
markers to the emulator's serial B log.  Wait for DONE and for rendering to
settle before capturing; a full-screen redraw can take several seconds.

The program disables echo and hides the cursor.  Capture the blank screen,
then type each key below and capture another image after output settles:

    s   scroll up more than one screen
    r   reverse scroll more than one screen
    i   insert/delete columns on every row
    e   fill with red, erase columns, erase rows
    c   show/hide/move cursor across every row

Every image must equal the initial blank image pixel for pixel.  The vertical
black-to-green gradient must stay at the same display coordinates.  This
detects moving wallpaper, incomplete erasure and cursor remnants.

Type t to draw text across the backdrop, including underline, reverse video,
and red background.  Check legibility and opaque red/reverse backgrounds.
Capture it, type m then c, and compare: restoring emulation mode after mapped
mode must reproduce the same text and background.  The extra c hides the
cursor that vcons enables on a full redraw.  Type b to clear, q to exit.

Also create/switch between wscons screens with wsconscfg, verify text and
decoration are restored, and return to screen 0 before removing test screens.
Creation/deletion uses zero-based indices (wsconscfg 1 creates ttyE1), but
the current wsconscfg -s/-g uses one-based VT numbers: -s 2 selects ttyE1
and -s 1 returns to ttyE0.
Boot a kernel without NEWPORT_DECOR to check the normal solid console path:
add "no options NEWPORT_DECOR" after including the generic configuration.
Physical Newport hardware remains necessary to qualify FIFO timing and the
cost of redrawing every character on scroll.

Validation on 2026-11-02
------------------------

Built mipseb kernels with and without NEWPORT_DECOR.  Ran the exerciser on
an emulated R4400 Indy with 256 MB RAM and Newport at 1282x1024 (160x64 text):

    Iris      a9b63183c130a23b199f82f01000b51cf70f26e2
    Prismatic cd1b0315b339bb859c4cad07bd0a5db2d89ceaf5

The initial background passed exact visible-pixel comparisons in both for
forward/reverse scrolling, column insertion/deletion, coloured fills and
erasure, cursor movement, and mapped-to-emulation restoration (with the
cursor hidden again).
Underline, reverse video and opaque coloured backgrounds were also inspected
in Prismatic's native framebuffer capture.
Prismatic also restored the exact text image after switching ttyE0 to ttyE1
and back (with the cursor hidden again).
The option-disabled kernel booted to a single-user shell with the normal
solid black console background in Prismatic.

This Iris build's iris-ci screenshot returned black images even at the PROM.
Its regression comparisons therefore used rex fbdump's ci.png, cropped to
the visible 1282x1024 pixels.  Prismatic captures checked the final RGB output.
No physical hardware or XZ decoration has been qualified.

The gradient update was rebuilt with and without NEWPORT_DECOR and checked
in Prismatic.  Its capture contains all 112 levels, with black at the top,
#008060 at the bottom, and monotonically increasing green/blue components.
Forward/reverse scrolling, column insertion/deletion, erasure, cursor
movement, and mode restoration again passed exact RGB image comparisons.

After enabling the scheme by default, both the default and explicitly
disabled mipseb kernels built successfully.  Generated configurations for
all three IP2x generic variants enable NEWPORT_DECOR and white kernel text.
